{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nThe MCP server's code workspace is cloned once and never updated, so gates read a tree that drifts from `main` indefinitely.\n\nThe workspace is docker volume `gerrit_mcp_code`, mounted at `/var/gerrit/site/mcp-code` and passed as both `MCP_CODE_DIR` and `REBAR_ROOT` (`infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh:390-417`). `infra/scripts/mcp-entrypoint.sh` treats a checkout as present when `.git` exists and `HEAD` resolves (`:127-130`), so `provision_code()` returns immediately for any present checkout (`:175-181`). Nothing ever fetches. `autodeploy.sh` advances a *separate* mirror at `/var/lib/rebar/mirror` (`:536-540`) and mounts this volume untouched (`:390-417`, `:852-960`).\n\nSo neither a container restart nor an image redeploy refreshes it. Once the clone succeeds, nothing in the system advances it again.\n\n## Evidence\n\nReflog on `i-00880b2c7f13527c5` shows a clone at `f1dd41a8f0` (2026-08-27) and thereafter only manual fast-forwards. No automated advance has ever occurred.\n\nAt the recorded incident the volume was 130 commits behind `origin/main`, and closing `f920-dc37-28e4-4a91` failed with 6 criteria reporting work absent from the tree — work that had in fact merged. The verifier said so directly: \"the current HEAD (665d1d0480...) does not contain these changes.\"\n\n## Impact\n\nA ticket whose work has genuinely merged cannot be closed, because the verifier cannot see it. The failure text invites the operator to re-record evidence or to `--force`, and both responses are wrong: the evidence is real and the tree is stale. Agents are forbidden from `--force`, so the correct-looking escape hatch is also unavailable.\n\nThe drift is unbounded and silent. Nothing reports it, and the gate output attributes the failure to the ticket rather than to the workspace.\n\n## Approach\n\nRefresh the workspace on the paths that already own its lifecycle, and make its drift observable instead of silent.\n\nAdd a refresh step to the provisioning path: take the existing checkout lock, require a clean tree, `git fetch origin main`, then `git merge --ff-only origin/main`. Run it for a checkout that is present, instead of returning early. A missing or unresolvable checkout keeps today's re-clone behaviour unchanged.\n\nA dirty or diverged workspace is never mutated — it fails loud and is left exactly as found. Silently resetting a workspace a gate may be mid-read on would trade one correctness bug for another, and it matches the existing `fail-loud-no-auto-restart` posture.\n\nThe refresh emits the workspace SHA and its distance from the target, so drift is visible before it causes a confusing gate verdict rather than after.\n\n**Portability.** The trigger is the MCP server's own lifecycle, which is where the workspace lives. That holds under any CI provider or none. A GitHub Actions workflow or a Gerrit hook as the only trigger would fail `project.portability`.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `infra/scripts/mcp-entrypoint.sh` — fetch and fast-forward an existing valid `MCP_CODE_DIR` rather than returning early.\n- Tests for the four refresh decisions.\n- `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md` — the workspace, how to check its drift, the manual fast-forward as break-glass.\n\n## Testing strategy\n\nTDD, failing test first in each case. Tests build a local repo whose `HEAD` is behind its remote and drive the provisioning path directly, with no container and no LLM call.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A clean `MCP_CODE_DIR` behind `origin/main` is fast-forwarded to the remote tip. Verified by a test that fails against today's early-return provisioning.\n- [ ] A dirty workspace is left byte-identical. Verified by comparing the working tree before the refusal against after it.\n- [ ] A diverged workspace is left byte-identical. Verified the same way, against a non-fast-forwardable history.\n- [ ] A refusal names its condition. Verified by asserting the failure text distinguishes dirty from diverged from unreachable.\n- [ ] A missing workspace is still re-cloned as today. Verified by a test that removes the checkout.\n- [ ] A workspace whose `HEAD` does not resolve is still re-cloned as today. Verified by a test that corrupts `HEAD`.\n- [ ] An unreachable remote leaves the workspace untouched. Verified by pointing the remote at a nonexistent path.\n- [ ] The refresh emits the workspace SHA with its distance from the target. Verified by reading the emitted diagnostics.\n- [ ] The refresh requires no single CI provider. Verified by reading the trigger path for any GitHub Actions or Gerrit dependency.\n- [ ] `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md` documents the workspace, the drift check, the break-glass. Verified by reading the file.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nThe certified gates' ref pinning and drift checking. The investigation for this ticket found that the close path re-resolves its pinned SHA with `fetch=False`, so a stale tree passes its own drift check — that is a separate defect with its own ticket, and it is not fixed by refreshing the workspace. Also out of scope: the gates' verdict logic, and the separate `/var/lib/rebar/mirror` used by `autodeploy.sh`."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1789784867730433866,"uuid":"b6dcdc20-0961-4176-a84f-7d38bda0ea24"}